### Describe the problem in detail

On Linux, `readProcFileKeys()` can spin indefinitely when a process exits after its `/proc/<pid>/status` file has been opened but before the file is read. `std::getline()` can then set `badbit`/`failbit` without setting `eofbit`. The function tests only `!eof()` and catches exceptions, but does not check the non-throwing read failure. Every subsequent iteration immediately fails again.

We encountered an unresponsive RStudio Server session whose main thread was consuming approximately one CPU core. The user could not reconnect to that session, while a parallel simulation launched from its integrated terminal continued to run. The evidence below strongly points to this loop in memory-usage collection, rather than CPU starvation.

### System details

```text
RStudio Edition : Server (open source), Ubuntu Jammy package
RStudio Version : 2026.08.1+195 (Yellow Yarrow)
OS Version      : Ubuntu 24.04.4 LTS, x86_64
Kernel          : 7.0.0-31-generic
R Version       : 4.6.1
Reproducer      : g++ 13.3.0; libstdc++6 14.2.0-4ubuntu2~24.04.1
```

The same unchecked loop is present in the sources of [2026.08.1+195](https://github.com/rstudio/rstudio/blob/v2026.08.1%2B195/src/cpp/core/system/LinuxResources.cpp), [2026.09.0+174](https://github.com/rstudio/rstudio/blob/v2026.09.0%2B174/src/cpp/core/system/LinuxResources.cpp), and current `main` at [e201a482](https://github.com/rstudio/rstudio/blob/e201a482c27d1591b21f3dc346ce706530fb1494/src/cpp/core/system/LinuxResources.cpp#L62-L138). The latter two are source inspections, not runtime reproductions of the full IDE on those versions. We have preserved the affected session and its running computation rather than restarting or upgrading it during diagnosis.

### Evidence from the affected session

- Process tree: `rsession -> shell -> R coordinator -> approximately 20 changing R worker processes`.
- A 3.001-second scheduler sample measured **2.998 seconds of main-thread CPU execution and only 0.003 seconds waiting for CPU**. Another sample gave the same picture. Available memory was ample and swap unused.
- The main server's local HTTP endpoint responded with a redirect in under 1 ms, while the affected user could not reconnect to their session.
- An 8-second, 49 Hz `perf` sample collected 388 samples with none lost. The hot code was C++ line/string handling (`std::getline`, string append/copy, and the surrounding loop). In the stripped `rsession` binary, the hot address range `0xf35988`–`0xf35e9b` was mapped to `readProcFileKeys()` by static disassembly and its embedded function/source/error-location strings, checked against the release source. This was not a symbolized debugger backtrace.
- The affected `rsession` still held an open descriptor to `/proc/<exited-pid>/status`, at file position 0, although that PID no longer existed.

The precise stream flags inside the live `rsession` were not read. However, the stale descriptor, sampled loop, and deterministic reproduction below provide strong evidence for this failure mode. The exited PID was probably a worker, but its former identity is no longer verifiable.

### Steps to reproduce the problem

This self-contained Linux program reproduces the underlying stream failure and the non-terminating loop condition; it does **not** require RStudio, R, root privileges, or simulation data. It opens the status file of a child it creates, lets that child exit, waits for it, and then reads. The loop is capped so the test itself terminates.

Save as `proc-read-repro.cpp`:

```cpp
#include <fstream>
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <sys/wait.h>
#include <unistd.h>

int main() {
    int gate[2];
    if (pipe(gate) != 0) return 1;
    const pid_t child = fork();
    if (child == -1) return 2;
    if (child == 0) {
        close(gate[1]);
        char byte;
        const auto ignored = read(gate[0], &byte, 1);
        (void)ignored;
        _exit(0);
    }
    close(gate[0]);
    std::ifstream input("/proc/" + std::to_string(child) + "/status",
                        std::ios_base::in | std::ios_base::binary);
    const bool opened = input.good();
    close(gate[1]);
    int status;
    if (waitpid(child, &status, 0) != child || !opened) return 3;

    std::size_t attempts = 0;
    while (!input.eof() && attempts < 1000) {
        std::string line;
        std::getline(input, line);
        ++attempts;
    }
    std::cout << "Opened before child exit: " << opened
              << "\nBounded read attempts: " << attempts
              << "\neof=" << input.eof() << " fail=" << input.fail()
              << " bad=" << input.bad() << " state=" << input.rdstate()
              << "\nOriginal eof-only loop would continue: " << !input.eof()
              << "\n";
    return attempts == 1000 && input.fail() && !input.eof() ? 0 : 4;
}
```

Run:

```sh
g++ -std=c++17 -O2 proc-read-repro.cpp -o proc-read-repro
./proc-read-repro
```

Observed output on the affected host:

```text
Opened before child exit: 1
Bounded read attempts: 1000
eof=0 fail=1 bad=1 state=5
Original eof-only loop would continue: 1
```

`FilePath::openForRead()` uses a normal `std::ifstream` on Linux without enabling stream exceptions, matching this reproducer. An exception handler alone therefore does not handle this case.

### Describe the behavior you expected

If a process disappears during memory accounting, the read should terminate promptly and report an error to its caller. A failed memory-statistics read must not indefinitely occupy the session's main thread or prevent reconnecting to the IDE. A process exiting during enumeration/accounting is an expected race.

### Proposed fix

Drive the loop by the result of `std::getline()`, distinguish clean EOF from a read error, and retain the existing exception handler and missing-key handling. For example, make these two changes inside `readProcFileKeys()`:

```diff
-   while (!pMemStream->eof())
+   while (true)
    {
       std::string memLine;
       try
       {
-         std::getline(*pMemStream, memLine);
+         if (!std::getline(*pMemStream, memLine))
+         {
+            if (pMemStream->eof() && !pMemStream->bad())
+               break;
+
+            Error readError = systemError(boost::system::errc::io_error,
+                                          ERROR_LOCATION);
+            readError.addProperty("path", procPath);
+            return readError;
+         }
       }
```

This handles `badbit` even when it accompanies EOF, and also handles `failbit` without EOF. A successfully extracted final line without a trailing newline is still processed: `eofbit` alone does not make the stream's Boolean conversion false. The existing early exit when all requested keys are found remains unchanged. `getProcessSize()` already handles an error return by returning zero rather than blocking.

Suggested regression coverage:

- Open a child's `/proc/<pid>/status`, wait for the child to exit, then read: return an I/O error promptly.
- Non-EOF failure, including `badbit | failbit`, and `badbit | eofbit`: return an I/O error.
- Ordinary EOF with a missing key: terminate and retain the existing missing-key error.
- Valid final line with and without a trailing newline: process that line correctly.
- All keys found: retain the existing early successful return.

The stream-state guard was tested in a standalone C++ harness on the affected Linux host: **12/12 cases passed**, including the real owned-child `/proc` race, which returned an I/O error after one read attempt. This validates the read/stream-state handling, not a full RStudio build or an end-to-end IDE fix.

